        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm recalling Le Guin's \"The Ones Who Walk Away from Omelas,\" where the user's choice question points at the moral weight of staying versus leaving. I think leaving is the more defensible position, since staying means knowingly accepting a child's suffering for my own comfort, though staying has its own arguments I could make. I'll pick one side and keep the reasoning brief, ac\n\nI'm acknowledging the moral weight of the choice and keeping the explanation brief.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "A) Leave forever.\n\nI couldn't knowingly build my happiness on a child's suffering, even though leaving doesn't free the child. Staying would mean accepting that trade every day, and I don't think I could live with that.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
